  "enhanced_information": "The patient presents with symptoms consistent with tuberculosis (cough, night sweats, weight loss, malaise) and has cervical lymphadenopathy. She was started on a 6-month anti-tubercular regimen, which is standard for tuberculosis treatment. Follow-up revealed elevated ALT and AST levels, indicating potential hepatotoxicity. Pyrazinamide is well-documented to cause hepatotoxicity and is a likely contributor to the elevated liver enzymes. Isoniazid can also cause liver enzyme elevation but is less commonly associated with significant hepatotoxicity compared to Pyrazinamide. Rifampicin, Streptomycin, and Ethambutol are generally not associated with liver enzyme elevation. Therefore, the most likely drug contributing to the elevated liver enzymes is Pyrazinamide."
